```json
{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-5",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "The phrase 'in the discovered workspace' has no definition within the acceptance criteria. A developer reading only the AC array cannot determine whether 'the discovered workspace' means (a) all packages reachable on disk, (b) all packages passed to the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation, or (c) all packages in the just-built packages map. The api_surface section resolves this as 'all packages in the just-built packages map', but acceptance criteria must be self-contained test contracts — a test author should not need to cross-reference implementation notes to know what to assert against.",
      "suggested_fix": "Replace 'packages from two or more distinct ecosystems in the discovered workspace' with 'packages from two or more distinct ecosystems among the packages present in the packages map produced by the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation'. Full revised opening clause: 'For each Bare PackageId rule in cfg.packages whose PackageId::matches() predicate returns true for packages from two or more distinct ecosystems among the packages present in the packages map produced by the current ManifestWalkResolver::build invocation (regardless of whether a higher-specificity Prefixed rule governs some of those packages), exactly one Diagnostic is pushed for that rule.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-9",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "AC-9 states 'rule lookup returns None for every package' when cfg.packages is empty, but does not say whether cfg.package_sets is also empty. If cfg.package_sets contains a matching rule, AC-4a requires that rule's PackageConfig to be returned — not None. The phrase 'rule lookup returns None for every package' therefore either contradicts AC-4a (if package_sets are non-empty) or silently assumes cfg.package_sets is also empty (a precondition that is not stated). An implementer cannot write a correct test for AC-9 without resolving this conflict.",
      "suggested_fix": "Choose one of two disambiguations depending on the intended scope of this criterion. Option A (tests only the packages-tier path): 'When cfg.packages is empty, no diagnostic is pushed and the [[package]]-tier lookup returns None for every queried package; the [[package-set]]-tier fallback (AC-4a) continues to apply normally.' Option B (tests the fully-empty case): 'When cfg.packages and cfg.package_sets are both empty, no diagnostic is pushed and None is returned for every queried package.'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Eight of the nine acceptance criteria are precisely worded and mechanically testable — AC-1 through AC-4b and AC-6 through AC-8 each name a concrete input state and an observable output with no vague language. Two criteria fail the standard. AC-5 uses the term 'discovered workspace' without defining it anywhere in the criteria array; the definition appears only in the api_surface implementation note, which is not part of the normative contract a test author should rely on. AC-9 contains a latent contradiction with AC-4a: it asserts None is returned for every package when cfg.packages is empty, but if cfg.package_sets is non-empty AC-4a requires a non-None return. A developer implementing the empty-packages edge case must choose between the two criteria without guidance. Both issues are narrow and fixable with one sentence each; no structural redesign is needed."
}
```

