## Audit: SPEC-004 (track-g-matrix-napi-maturin.json)

I read the spec, the three sibling specs (track-b, track-e, track-f — no scope overlap found, they touch `apply.rs`/`walk.rs`/`aggregate.rs`/`resolve.rs`, entirely disjoint from matrix/napi/python territory), the source design doc `callisto-matrix.md`, and spot-checked the codebase claims directly (`crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs`, `crates/callisto-manifests/src/python.rs`, `crates/callisto-cli/src/cli.rs`, `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`, `crates/callisto-model/src/exec.rs`, `crates/callisto-cli/src/error.rs`, `crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs`).

**Premise claims verified accurate**: the 18-triple list in `triple_to_role` (napi.rs:147-166) matches the spec's list exactly (and correctly diverges from the stale design-doc table, which the spec explicitly and correctly calls out). No existing reader for `[tool.maturin].targets` or `engines.node`. `requires-python` only appears in a test-fixture string, not a real reader. `Command::Matrix` does not exist in cli.rs. No `MatrixReport`/`PlatformTargetGroup`/etc. types exist anywhere in the workspace. The action.yml stub `{"platformTargets":{}}` is real (line 161) and its declared output description ("JSON array...") already contradicts the current object-shaped fallback — the spec's AC-016 fix is well-grounded.

However, I found concrete defects that would stop or misdirect an implementer, most seriously two API-surface claims that don't match the actual code shape and would not compile as written.

```json
{
  "issues": [
    {
      "criterion_id": "api_surface:callisto_graph::commands::matrix",
      "type": "incomplete",
      "description": "The signature `pub fn matrix<R: CommandRunner>(ws: &Workspace, opts: &MatrixOptions) -> Result<MatrixReport, CliError>` returns CliError, but CliError is defined in crates/callisto-cli/src/error.rs, which depends on callisto-graph (Cargo.toml: callisto-cli -> callisto-graph), not the reverse. callisto-graph cannot name CliError. Every sibling graph-layer command (e.g. crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/status.rs: `pub fn status<R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver>(...) -> Result<StatusReport, GraphError>`) returns GraphError, which CliError::Graph(#[from] GraphError) then wraps at the CLI layer.",
      "suggested_fix": "Change the signature to `pub fn matrix<R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver>(ws: &Workspace<'_, R, D>, opts: &MatrixOptions) -> Result<MatrixReport, GraphError>`, matching the status()/plan_publish() pattern. The CLI handler in crates/callisto-cli/src/commands/matrix.rs converts GraphError to CliError via the existing `?`/From impl, exactly as the other command handlers do."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "api_surface:callisto_graph::commands::matrix",
      "type": "incomplete",
      "description": "`ws: &Workspace` is used with zero generic arguments. The real type is `pub struct Workspace<'a, R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver = ManifestWalkResolver>` (crates/callisto-graph/src/lib.rs:51) — it requires a lifetime and an R argument (D defaults). `&Workspace` alone will not compile.",
      "suggested_fix": "Write the parameter as `ws: &Workspace<'_, R, D>` (or `&Workspace<'_, R>` if relying on D's default), consistent with how status()/plan_publish() declare it."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-010",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "AC-010 covers malformed TOML in pyproject.toml, but no criterion covers a malformed package.json (invalid JSON syntax) encountered while reading napi.targets or engines.node. Since napi/maturin/npm/pypi are all read in the same matrix() pass, a developer has no specified behavior for the JSON-side parse failure — is it the same GraphError/exit-1 path, and what does the error message name (path vs. field)?",
      "suggested_fix": "Add an AC-010b: 'Running `callisto matrix --format json` against a workspace containing a package.json with malformed JSON syntax (e.g. a trailing comma) exits with code 1 and prints an error identifying the unreadable/malformed manifest path; no MatrixReport JSON is printed to stdout.' mirroring AC-010's structure exactly."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-001,AC-002",
      "type": "missing-error-case",
      "description": "No criterion specifies behavior when a single package declares both napi.targets (package.json) and [tool.maturin].targets (pyproject.toml) in the same registered package. PlatformTargetGroup.kind is a single enum value per package-keyed map entry (BTreeMap<String, PlatformTargetGroup>), so it's undefined whether this is an error, whether one source silently wins, or whether the map key collides and overwrites silently.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add an explicit criterion, e.g.: 'When a single registered package has both a napi.targets declaration and a [tool.maturin].targets declaration, callisto matrix returns Err(GraphError::...) naming the package and both conflicting sources; this is a hard error, not a silent last-write-wins.' Or, if dual-toolchain packages are legitimate, specify the disambiguation key (e.g. two synthetic map keys `pkgname` and `pkgname:maturin`) explicitly rather than leaving it to the implementer."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-011",
      "type": "incomplete",
      "description": "The spec introduces a new DiagnosticCode variant ('DiagnosticCode::UnrecognisedPlatformTriple or equivalent') but the scope has no requirement to add a row to the diagnostic attribution table at docs/01-spec.md §G.13, unlike track-e (SPEC-002), which treats that table update as a hard AC (AC-11) for its new BareRuleMatchesMultipleEcosystems variant. The DiagnosticCode enum (crates/callisto-model/src/diagnostic.rs:91) is documented at docs/01-spec.md:8315 '### G.13 Diagnostic attribution map' as the canonical registry; leaving a variant out of it is an established repo convention violation, not a stylistic choice.",
      "suggested_fix": "Add to scope: 'Add a row to the diagnostic attribution table at docs/01-spec.md §G.13 for the new DiagnosticCode variant, with Emitted-by referencing the matrix module, and appropriate escalated_by/governed_by placeholders,' and pin the exact variant name (see next finding) so the doc row and the code agree."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "api_surface:callisto_graph::commands::matrix",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "'new DiagnosticCode::UnrecognisedPlatformTriple or equivalent' leaves the exact variant name (and therefore its serde kebab-case wire value) to the implementer's discretion, with no AC pinning it. Every existing DiagnosticCode variant has an exact, spec-fixed name (see SPEC-002's AC-10, which fixes the exact string 'bare-rule-matches-multiple-ecosystems'). Without a fixed name here, two implementers following this spec could diverge, and any downstream consumer (CI, docs) has nothing stable to match against.",
      "suggested_fix": "Fix the exact name: 'Add `DiagnosticCode::UnrecognisedPlatformTriple` to crates/callisto-model/src/diagnostic.rs (kebab-case serde value \"unrecognised-platform-triple\")' — remove 'or equivalent' and add an AC asserting the serialized string, mirroring SPEC-002 AC-10."
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "AC-016",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "'(or an equivalent shell pipeline producing the same jq expression)' admits two interpretations: an implementer could read this as 'the exact jq filter text must appear verbatim' or 'any pipeline that is behaviorally equivalent for some restructuring of the shell code is acceptable' (e.g. splitting into two steps, using `jq -c`, reformatting the filter with different whitespace/quoting). Since this is the only AC governing action.yml and it's a hard AC (not is_error_case), the acceptance bar needs to be mechanically checkable.",
      "suggested_fix": "State the check precisely: 'action.yml's NATIVE_MATRIX assignment line, after `jq` is applied to the output of `callisto matrix --format json`, is byte-for-byte parseable as the same jq program `[.platformTargets[].targets[]] | unique_by(.artifactName)` (whitespace-insensitive); the shell wrapper (variable names, quoting style) may differ, but the jq filter argument must not.'"
    },
    {
      "criterion_id": "scope",
      "type": "ambiguous",
      "description": "Scope says the reading/assembly logic lives in a new module 'crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs', but api_surface names the public function 'callisto_graph::commands::matrix' — implying crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs, alongside status.rs/publish.rs/etc. It's unstated whether both files should exist (matrix.rs holding read/derive logic, commands/matrix.rs a thin CommandRunner-generic wrapper calling into it — mirroring how commands/publish.rs calls into apply.rs/groups.rs), or whether 'crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs' is just imprecise phrasing for the single commands/matrix.rs file.",
      "suggested_fix": "State explicitly: 'crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs contains the manifest-reading and MatrixReport-assembly logic (private helpers); crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs contains the public `pub fn matrix<R: CommandRunner, D: DependencyResolver>(...)` entry point that calls into matrix.rs, mirroring the commands/publish.rs -> apply.rs/groups.rs pattern.'"
    }
  ],
  "overall": "fail",
  "reasoning": "Codebase premise claims (triple table, absence of maturin/engines.node readers, absence of MatrixReport types, absence of Command::Matrix, action.yml stub) all check out against the real source. No scope overlap with track-b/e/f. But the api_surface for the core graph-layer function has two compile-blocking errors (CliError used across a reverse-dependency boundary; Workspace referenced without its required generics) that a developer would hit immediately and have to guess the fix for — that alone fails the 'implement without asking a question' bar. Additional gaps: asymmetric error-case coverage (TOML malformed but not JSON malformed), unspecified dual-napi/maturin-declaration behavior, an unfixed DiagnosticCode variant name with no doc-table update requirement (breaking an established repo convention from SPEC-002), and two ambiguous 'or equivalent' escape hatches (AC-016's jq pipeline, module placement) that leave implementation-critical decisions unstated."
}
```

